'''asparamide''' (as-par′a̤-mid), ''n.'' [< ''aspar(agin)'' + ''amide.''] Same as ''asparagin.''

'''asparginic''' (as-pär-jin′ik), ''a.'' [< ''aspar(a)gin'' + ''-ic.''] Same as ''aspartic.''

'''asparmate''' (as-pär′māt), ''n.'' [< ''aspar(a)m(ide)'' + ''-ate''<sup>1</sup>.] Same as ''aspartate.''

'''aspartate''' (as-pär′tāt), ''n.'' [< ''aspart(ic)'' + ''-ate''<sup>1</sup>.] Any salt of aspartic acid.

'''aspartic''' (as-pär′tik), ''a.'' [< ''aspar(agin)'' + ''-t-ic.''] Pertaining to or obtained from asparagin. Also ''asparagic, asparginic.''—{{smaller|'''Aspartic acid,''' C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>7</sub>NO<sub>4</sub>, a crystalline acid derived from asparagin.}}

'''aspe†''', ''n.'' An old spelling of ''asp''<sup>1</sup> and ''asp''<sup>2</sup>.

'''aspect''' (as′pekt, formerly as-pekt′), ''n.'' [< ME. ''aspect'', < L. ''aspectus'', seeing, look, appearance, countenance, < ''aspicere'', look, behold, < ''ad'', to, + ''specere'', look: see ''species'' and ''spy.''] '''1.''' The act of seeing, or of looking at anything; view; gaze; glance; look. [Archaic.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Some other mistress hath thy sweet ''aspects.''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', C. of E., ii. 2.}}
His ''aspect'' was bent on the ground. {{right|''Scott.''}}
Meeting the cold ''aspect'' of Duty.
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Autocrat, xi.}}}}
'''2.''' Countenance; look or particular appearance of the face; mien; air: as, a mild or severe ''aspect.''
{{smaller block|Wiser princes patron the arts, and carry an indulgent ''aspect'' unto scholars. {{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Religio Medici, ii. 3.}}
<poem>Yet, had his ''aspect'' nothing of severe,
But such a face as promis’d him sincere.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Character of Good Parson, i. 12.}}}}
'''3.''' Appearance to the eye or mind; look: as, the physical ''aspect'' of the country.
{{smaller block|<poem>And then our arms, like to a muzzled bear,</poem>}}

readings.c2s4:
{{smaller block|Save in ''aspect'', have all offence seal’d up.
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, ii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>How sweet, how fair, and lovely her ''aspects'' are!
Her eyes, like bright Eoan flames, shoot through me.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'' (''and another''?), Prophetess, iii. 3.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|What a collegiate ''aspect'' has that fine Elizabethan hall, where the fountain plays!
{{right|''Lamb'', Old Benchers.}}}}
'''4.''' One of the ways in which a thing may be viewed or contemplated: as, to present an object or a subject in its true ''aspect''; in a double ''aspect''; a favorable ''aspect''.
{{smaller block|<poem>Something loftier, more adorned,
Than is the common ''aspect'', daily garb,
Of human life. {{right|''Wordsworth'', Prelude, v.}}</poem>
Undoubtedly we have a right to make new words, as they are needed by the fresh ''aspects'' under which life presents itself here in the New World; and, indeed, wherever a language is alive, it grows.
{{right|''Lowell'', Introd. to Biglow Papers, 1st ser.}}}}
'''5.''' Practical bearing or reference. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|The ''aspect'' of atonement is obviously toward creatures, working effects on them, not on God.
{{right|''J. Gilbert'', Christ. Atonement, p. 167. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}
'''6.''' View commanded; prospect; outlook.
{{smaller block|This town has a good ''aspect'' toward the hill from whence we descended. {{right|''Evelyn.''}}
[Now used in this sense mainly with reference to the points of the compass: as, a house has a southern ''aspect'' or exposure.]}}
'''7.''' In ''astrol.'', the relative positions of the planets as they appear at any given time to an observer upon the earth; the combined look of the heavenly bodies from the earth. {{smaller|The aspects are nine in number: (1) semisextile, a difference of longitude of 30°; (2) semisquare, of 45°; (3) sextile, of 60°; (4) quintile, of 72°; (5) square or quartile, of 90°; (6) trine, of 120°; (7) sesquiquadrate, of 135°; (8) biquintile, of 144°; (9) opposition, of 180°. To these may be added conjunction, which occurs when the planets have the same longitude. ''Good aspects'' are the semisextile, sextile, quintile, trine, and biquintile. ''Bad aspects'' are the semisquare, square, sesquiquadrate, and opposition. ''Mundane as-''}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller|''pects'' are such as are formed by the houses in horary astrology and by the semiarcs of the planets in nativities.}}
{{smaller block|The glorious planet, Sol, . . . whose med′cinable eye
Corrects the ill ''aspects'' of planets evil.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. and C., i. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>We, that behold the sad ''aspects'' of heaven,
Leading sense-blinded men, feel grief enough
To know, though not to speak, their miseries.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Thierry and Theodoret, iii. 3.}}}}
'''8.''' In ''her.'', the position of an animal with reference to the spectator.—{{smaller|'''Ambulacral aspect.''' See ''ambulacral.''—'''Aspect of a plane,''' in ''math.'', the direction of its normal.—'''In full aspect.''' Same as ''affronté'', 2.—'''In trian aspect,''' in a position between ''affronté'' and ''passant.''—'''Mesial aspect.''' See ''mesial.''}}

'''aspect†''' (as-pekt′), ''v. t.'' [< L. ''aspectare'', look at, view, freq. of ''aspicere'', look at: see ''aspect'', ''n.''] To behold; look upon.
{{smaller block|<poem>Happy in their mistakes those people whom
The northern pole ''aspects.''</poem>
{{right|''Sir W. Temple'', tr. of Lucan, in Heroic Virtue.}}}}

'''aspectable''' (as-pek′tạ-bl), ''a.'' [< L. ''aspectabilis'', that may be seen, < ''aspectare'', see, look at: see ''aspect'', ''v.''] '''1.''' Capable of being seen; visible.
{{smaller block|What is in this ''aspectable'' world? {{right|''Ray'', Creation.}}}}
'''2.''' Fair or fit to be seen.
{{smaller block|<poem>Via Vittoria, the ''aspectable'' street
Where he lived mainly.</poem>
{{right|''Browning'', Ring and Book, I. 57.}}}}
[Rare in both senses.]

'''aspectant''' (as-pek′tant), ''a.'' [< L. ''aspectan(t-)s'', ppr. of ''aspectare'': see ''aspect'', ''v.''] In ''her.'', same as ''affronté'', 2.

'''aspected''' (as-pek′ted), ''p. a.'' [< ''aspect'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] '''1†.''' Looked at; viewed.—'''2.''' Having an aspect or look. [Rare.]
